ligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 144\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{12}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "166cd7e4-5568-5769-8bc6-4ffc4ed8b508" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Matrix of a Dilation Given a Vector and its Image"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebd1c492-bc34-536a-93af-528535ac0dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393829} {:answer/id #uuid "2527ce77-64be-5a89-9267-c574afcbee7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393830} {:answer/id #uuid "83949906-5947-5c67-8c2d-a083cec8ad9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393831} {:answer/id #uuid "868da73a-ea9a-5f95-989a-9c024bc284cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393832} {:answer/id #uuid "f66fdf6a-68cd-54e5-81ef-28e0fbde30f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f66fdf6a-68cd-54e5-81ef-28e0fbde30f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393833} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da0ed2f-51d0-5d07-b631-9e8cc54faa32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba67f8b5-b4aa-5dbc-8d3f-1501ac2571d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78295" :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-54 \\\\ 81\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $T(v)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-54 \\\\ 81\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $T(v)=\\frac{1}{9}v,$ and so the linear transformation $T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k=\\frac{1}{9}.$\n\nThe matrix that represents a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $T$ is\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Nilpotent Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388a6e36-47ac-5d37-98c8-6a8422650b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -25 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393836} {:answer/id #uuid "b87b90b7-2463-5e8e-ad19-a99c7fc4f432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393837} {:answer/id #uuid "bba036bc-c4e5-5518-bbe9-5b2cb2b09056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393838} {:answer/id #uuid "0d78787e-c53a-504e-b15a-57da10dcc04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393839} {:answer/id #uuid "6e62e811-4b5f-5978-9b25-4900d2227f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b87b90b7-2463-5e8e-ad19-a99c7fc4f432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393837} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb48b9b-e45b-5d41-b39e-ff8551f772bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "578bb254-91a1-5ec9-b189-2ecd517094f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78344" :question/problem "If $B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]$, then ${B}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${B}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 25 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${B}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $B$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${B}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{6} & ={B}^{2}\\cdot {B}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {B}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{6}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f69ec878-4b9a-5539-8725-c4b325a49f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{2}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393843} {:answer/id #uuid "29d6e8f7-1219-53bf-a56c-456c94e0e5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{1}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393844} {:answer/id #uuid "3db98340-dc8e-5a06-8c2d-d1c9576c6c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393845} {:answer/id #uuid "24de4534-9c4a-5a84-9729-1290e51ab1ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{50}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{25}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393846} {:answer/id #uuid "8be4381f-753d-5f66-b1b1-28c473efbdb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j+\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3db98340-dc8e-5a06-8c2d-d1c9576c6c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393845} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d079c9-27cf-5f55-a153-92a8cb90ae4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72f93e03-a33f-50d8-b52b-f8964fad8ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-78349" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=t\\,i+\\sqrt{8}t\\,j-4t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=5t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=5t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{5}.$  \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{s}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+(\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\frac{s}{5})j-4\\cdot \\frac{s}{5}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}s}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4s}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{5})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{8}s}{5})j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{5})k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{8}}{5}\\,j-\\frac{4}{5}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aecbb8b-bda3-5956-9b0f-98509330a84d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc4c695-abde-5448-ab84-839794d4b9a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393850} {:answer/id #uuid "82715f51-4266-5316-ba54-15a96b26aea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{4}⁡(s)\\,i+{sin}^{4}⁡(s)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393851} {:answer/id #uuid "986a4e80-ffd7-5103-99bd-9d5fbf8fa7ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡({s}^{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡({s}^{4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393852} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeedef8-6d70-5a50-8672-9a39d5a5f8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393853} {:answer/id #uuid "12c6fdf5-7cf6-53c1-8053-8f7bd4a787ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({s}^{3})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({s}^{3})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeedef8-6d70-5a50-8672-9a39d5a5f8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393853} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e7a5ea-46f6-52ca-814b-b0356c2e00b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d899d29b-8ceb-54bb-99b6-823abccec15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78391" :question/problem "What is the parametrization of the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{4})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{4})\\,j,$  $\\,t\\ge 0,$ by its arc length $s?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{4}))i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{4}))j \\\\  & =-4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{4})\\,i+4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{4})\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{[-4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{4})]}^{2}+{[4{t}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{4})]}^{2}} \\\\  & =4|t{|}^{3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡({t}^{4})+{cos}^{2}⁡({t}^{4})} \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the arc length of the curve from $0$ to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}4{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={u}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $s={t}^{4}$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{4} & =s \\\\ t & =\\sqrt[4]{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector function that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\sqrt[4]{s}) \\\\  & =cos\\,((\\sqrt[4]{s})^{4})\\,i+sin\\,((\\sqrt[4]{s})^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(s)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(s)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7343e3-fa7d-58c8-b4c7-49ef5b85110b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Unit Tangent Given the Parametrization of a Curve by Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393857} {:answer/id #uuid "90720291-67d0-5e1a-b546-8a1aea34b932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393858} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcf0b34-e534-51fc-97fb-64d7d6fafa02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393859} {:answer/id #uuid "07fc2226-9b81-5440-8648-0026a95bbbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{18},\\,\\frac{2}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393860} {:answer/id #uuid "edbdf1ca-35ec-595a-a27c-e608c973c72f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{81},\\,\\frac{4}{81},\\,\\frac{8}{81}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f744694c-a7e5-531b-b1a0-b70a89880b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393857} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fefcfb2-453a-5854-be55-e93605da6418" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56897adb-706a-5c81-8930-6f2aa15c0be7" :question/math-academy-id "q-78404" :question/problem "Given the curve  $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{2},\\,8{t}^{2}⟩,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=9{t}^{2},$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=9{t}^{2}$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{s}{9} \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3}) \\\\  & =⟨{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,4{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2},\\,8{(\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{3})}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{s}{9},\\,\\frac{4s}{9},\\,\\frac{8s}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4s}{9}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{8s}{9})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{9},\\,\\frac{4}{9},\\,\\frac{8}{9}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d0cda54-4a4b-5255-b375-ce5e7b604dff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Matrix Equation Using Algebraic Manipulation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "366c6653-367a-59a7-84cb-6ee9c7105259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=BA\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393864} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdfd0ed-3fe5-5b86-87d3-32bcacf5d620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={B}^{-1}\\,d\\,{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393865} {:answer/id #uuid "14be786b-6205-5224-9e4e-5339226f55dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393866} {:answer/id #uuid "5efc90ca-87fe-57c3-ba7c-64fc159ed6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393867} {:answer/id #uuid "3969bfd8-042f-5db5-aa6d-bb90db966f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={A}^{-1}\\,d\\,B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5efc90ca-87fe-57c3-ba7c-64fc159ed6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393867} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e367af9-44e1-5186-9afd-a825a6fad5d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecb9056c-9932-5a89-8f17-1df1e929dfe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-78445" :question/problem "Consider a matrix equation $AB\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $4\\times 4$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $4\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $AB\\,x=d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB\\,x & =d \\\\ {A}^{-1}AB\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e970fe-cff8-50e3-9b94-67ad97b03da7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d718e860-d9b8-5907-8e53-05046524911e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 14 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393871} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2da8db-419b-5fc2-a2f6-c5d25d2ee8d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393872} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c7ef1-9d5b-59ed-9bfc-b71aaba92cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393873} {:answer/id #uuid "41965806-1f4e-5183-9b81-5ddeff998ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & 12 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393874} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1e5569-d116-55e7-87e8-e2b89abc51e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1e5569-d116-55e7-87e8-e2b89abc51e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393875} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d67059-f6d7-5521-9126-3196f2ed6789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88934d15-ae66-5c80-a236-6a63bba3d33b" :question/math-academy-id "q-78497" :question/problem "If $F=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${F}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${F}^{2},$ we multiply $F$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{2} & =F\\cdot F \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 4 & 19\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2bbdd76-6c28-529e-93aa-9c2d32cfcca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175565} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6c3bdd-0c01-51b5-ab08-24122bea76d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175566} {:answer/id #uuid "06b6607a-5d73-5017-8a01-9b4dc5457949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175567} {:answer/id #uuid "4c87c5e8-8384-57d9-918b-e9f1dd1c836b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175568} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2bcb8b-1587-5fae-b789-cef3d9a309c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175569}] :answer-field/id #uuid "749e8cda-014b-5ec3-b57d-ede6636600d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175564}] :question/id #uuid "22e82ef5-ade9-5251-8d75-4be1c8a1c5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-78501" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$ for the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$(A - 2I)^{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc8dc1a1-9606-5b67-942e-219ab2ddfc1c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Power of a Nilpotent Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46eb92c2-7586-5474-8de1-8f3018443d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393878} {:answer/id #uuid "533dd821-5655-5510-a57f-8493e82642af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393879} {:answer/id #uuid "aef19fc2-859f-59e3-8cd0-62db907c84bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393880} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c8ed65-2963-576c-8a72-8223e203e5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393881} {:answer/id #uuid "d00a9629-7e88-5d9d-aadb-bf40609cc5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -16 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aef19fc2-859f-59e3-8cd0-62db907c84bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393880} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a06aef-9285-5bae-89f6-500d7ffcb21a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1700d4e7-644c-5246-9a39-552cdcd23dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-78534" :question/problem "If $K=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${K}^{6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Computing ${K}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{2} & =K\\cdot K \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${K}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $K$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${K}^{6}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{6} & ={K}^{2}\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\cdot {K}^{4} \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{6}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393883 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12637b1d-ca03-568f-b1fc-6a626eec88de" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Given the Inverse of the Coefficient Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e5fd665-92c8-5de9-a962-94e1f5665e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393885} {:answer/id #uuid "b49b5346-149e-5688-b4e3-abd91ac82b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393886} {:answer/id #uuid "dde44d62-1c85-5ae3-b2b1-d15a071a6e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393887} {:answer/id #uuid "86f61021-d77c-5497-9ea7-8d2d27863b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393888} {:answer/id #uuid "6286448b-0a3f-5139-a73d-05652685e000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6286448b-0a3f-5139-a73d-05652685e000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393889} :answer-field/id #uuid "4352c172-b7da-52e6-b834-2727aa018a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c98c808b-049b-59b0-8d36-61df2339933c" :question/math-academy-id "q-78549" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+4z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+9z=0 \\\\ y+6z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -8 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-3,z=1.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-3)+1=-2.$"}]}
